.TH LATTICEWM\-CONFIG 5 "2026-08-02" "LatticeWM 0.1.0" "File Formats"
.SH NAME
latticewm\-config \- configuring and extending LatticeWM
.SH SYNOPSIS
.I ~/.config/latticewm/init.lisp
.SH DESCRIPTION
LatticeWM is configured by a file of plain Common Lisp, read in the
.B LATTICEWM/USER
package \(em which inherits the model, the policy surface and the runtime, so
nothing in it needs a package prefix.
.PP
.B latticewm \-\-write\-config
writes a commented starter file.
.B latticewm \-\-check\-config
loads it, reports every problem, and exits non\-zero if there were any; run
that before logging out rather than after.
.PP
An error in the file is logged and startup continues.  A window manager that
refused to run because of a typo in its configuration would leave you with no
way to fix the typo.
.PP
*This page is a map, not a reference.*  The reference is generated from the
running program and cannot go stale:
.PP
.RS
.B latticewm \-\-list\-options
.br
.B latticewm \-\-list\-commands
.br
.B latticewm \-\-list\-keys
.br
.B latticewm \-\-extension\-surface
.RE
.PP
Every option and every generic carries a paragraph explaining what it decides
and what turning it off costs you.  Those paragraphs are the specification.
.SH THE FOUR TIERS
.TP
.B Tier 0 \(em a value
.B (setf *gaps* 8)
\&.  No restart, and the only tier available to somebody who does not write
Lisp.  Every fork the design argued about appears here as a value rather than
as a branch buried in a method.
.TP
.B Tier 1 \(em a method
.B (defmethod gaps ((p conventional-policy) container) 8)
\&.  One decision changed.  Specialize on
.BR conventional\-policy ,
not on a protocol class: the shipped defaults are on the protocol classes, and
yours must be more specific or it replaces rather than extends them.  Use
.B call\-next\-method
when you mean \(lqthe usual thing, plus this\(rq.
.TP
.B Tier 2 \(em a method plus state
Every node, window, output and overlay carries a
.B props
plist.  Use it rather than adding a slot to a class you do not own.
.TP
.B Tier 3 \(em a new class
A new container kind, or a whole policy.  The lattice is one of each and is a
separate ASDF system that touches no core file; a build gate asserts that on
every build.
.SH THE SIX PROTOCOLS
.B policy
is not one interface.  It is a class implementing six, and the shipped defaults
live on the narrow ones \(em so a mixin that answers for exactly one is a real
thing you can write and says what it is.
.TP
.B layout\-policy
How a rectangle is divided, and what is shown.
.TP
.B appearance\-policy
Colours, fonts, and what the status line says.
.TP
.B motion\-policy
Where the cursor goes.
.TP
.B structure\-policy
Where things go when the tree changes.
.TP
.B lifecycle\-policy
Windows arriving, leaving, floating, minimizing.
.TP
.B input\-policy
What a key, a click or a drag means.
.SH THE VALUES PEOPLE CHANGE FIRST
.TP
.B *terminal* *editor* *browser* *file-manager*
What the launcher commands run.
.TP
.B *modifier*
The modifier every default binding hangs off, :SUPER by default.  Change this
one value and the whole keymap moves with it.
.\" CLAIM: (eq :super (option-default :modifier))
.TP
.B *xkb-layout* *xkb-variant* *xkb-options* *xkb-model* *xkb-rules*
The keyboard layout itself, e.g. \(lqde\(rq or \(lqus,de\(rq.  This changes
what the keys do for every application on the machine.  On river there is
nothing else on the system that sets it \(em no settings daemon, no
.BR xinput (1)
\(em so if you do not type on a US keyboard this is the line to set.  NIL, the
default, leaves whatever river started with.  Needs
.BR xkbcli (1),
which ships with libxkbcommon.
.TP
.B *keyboard-layout*
A different thing with a similar name: the
.I shift map,
one of \(lqus\(rq, \(lquk\(rq, \(lqde\(rq, \(lqfr\(rq, \(lqdvorak\(rq or
\(lqcolemak\(rq.  River does the xkb work and does not tell us the
.I shifted
keysym, so the shifted glyph has to be declared rather than derived, or the
window manager's own prompt inserts American punctuation on a German keyboard.
Setting
.B *xkb-layout*
adopts the matching table automatically, so in the ordinary case there is
nothing to do here.
.TP
.B *tap-to-click* *natural-scroll* *accel-speed* *accel-profile*
.TP
.B *click-method* *scroll-method* *scroll-button* *disable-while-typing*
.TP
.B *repeat-rate* *repeat-delay* *scroll-factor* *left-handed*
Input device settings, applied to every device that supports them.  A device
that does not support one is skipped rather than sent it.
.TP
.B *input-rules*
Per\-device overrides, as a list of (MATCHER . PLIST).  MATCHER is T, a device
kind (:KEYBOARD :POINTER :TOUCH :TABLET), a substring of the device name, or a
function of one device.  Every matching rule applies in order and a later one
wins, so a rule for T is a default and a rule naming a device is an override.
A laptop has a touchpad
.I and
a mouse and they want opposite things, which is what this exists for.
.RS
.PP
.B (setf *input-rules* '(("Touchpad" :natural-scroll t)))
.RE
.PP
.B (list-inputs)
prints every device present with its name, its kind, what it supports and what
is currently in force.
.TP
.B *gaps* *outer-gaps* *border-width* *smart-gaps*
Pixels between panes, between the layout and the screen edge, and around a
window.  With
.B *smart-gaps*
on \(em the default \(em the outer gap and the border are dropped when a single
pane is alone on a screen, because there is nothing there for either to
separate.  An empty pane beside a window is a second pane and keeps both.
.\" CLAIM: (eq t (option-default :smart-gaps))
.\" CLAIM: (option-readers :smart-gaps)
.IP
The first three share a name with a policy generic, and the relationship is
always the same: the generic is the extension point and the option is what its
shipped method returns.  A policy that overrides
.B gaps
stops reading
.BR *gaps* .
.B latticewm \-\-extension\-surface
prints, under every option, what in the running program reads it.
.TP
.B *focused-border-color* *unfocused-border-color* *cursor-border-color*
Four straight\-alpha floats each.  You write the colour you mean and the wire
layer premultiplies.
.TP
.B *spawn-mode* *split-axis* *focus-after-close*
Where a new window goes, how a fresh split is cut, and where the cursor lands
after a close.
.TP
.B *window-rules*
Declarative per\-window overrides, matched on app id, title or parent, and
setting any of float, workspace, path, focus, fullscreen, minimize, border
colour, border width, decoration, capabilities, tags or label.  An unknown key
is reported rather than ignored.
.SH KEYS
A binding names a command as a
.I string,
so redefining the command later takes effect without rebinding the key.
.PP
.RS
.B (define\-key *keymap* "Super+Return" '("terminal"))
.RE
.PP
Both
.B +
and
.B \-
separate a chord, and the usual aliases work: super/mod4/logo, ctrl/control/C,
alt/mod1/meta/M.  Binding a key to a
.B keymap
makes it a chord prefix; pressing the prefix alone lists what the second key
can be.
.SH HOOKS
A generic decides
.I what happens
\(em one answer wins, and the return value matters.  A hook notices
.I that something happened
\(em every function runs, and nobody is in charge.  If you want a hook's return
value, you wanted a method.
.PP
.RS
.B (add\-hook :window\-opened 'note\-the\-window)
.RE
.PP
.B Give the function a name and add the symbol,
not
.B #'the\-function
and not a lambda.  A function object is a snapshot: redefining the function
afterwards leaves the hook calling the old one, and adding it again adds a
second copy.  A symbol has neither problem.
.PP
.B (all\-hooks)
lists every declared hook with its documentation.  One of them,
.BR :reserve\-space ,
is additive: each function answers how much of an output to keep clear and the
answers are summed, so two status bars each get their strip.
.SH THE CONTROL SOCKET
A Unix socket in
.BR $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR ,
mode 0600, reachable only by the user who owns the session.  The binary is its
own client:
.PP
.RS
.B latticewm \-\-eval '(workspace 3)'
.RE
.PP
Forms run in the window manager's own thread, so they may touch the compositor.
The protocol is write a form, read a line, so anything that can open a socket
can drive it.
.PP
A SWANK server is available and is
.B off by default
\(em see
.B *swank\-port*
and
.BR *swank\-interface* .
It offers arbitrary code execution with no authentication step, which is right
for a development tool and wrong for something that starts before the user has
done anything.
.\" CLAIM: (null (option-default :swank-port))
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.config/latticewm/init.lisp
The configuration file.
.TP
.I ~/.config/latticewm/
Also on ASDF's registry, so an extension of your own dropped here as a system
is loadable by name with
.BR (load\-extension "name") .
.TP
.I PREFIX/share/latticewm/
Installed extensions, examples and documentation.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR latticewm (1)
.PP
.I PREFIX/share/latticewm/doc/EXTENDING.org
is the guide, with worked examples.
.SH AUTHOR
Shaul Khayyat.
